[Music]  as millions recover from kogit 19  economies around the world are still  exhibiting signs of ill health  global trade remains disrupted prices  are rising and for many goods and  services demand is far outstripping  supply  so what should governments do to help  aid economic recovery  and after covert 19 how can economic  reform help to change the world for the  better  our experts answer your questions  after all the stimulus provided by  governments will we see hyperinflation  or a period of depression  so i i would say that neither of those  are very likely possibilities or  certainly hyperinflation is extremely  unlikely that would require the sort of  collapse of  of government and there's no reason to  think that the stimulus we've had during  the pandemic is going to lead to that  sort of  failed state situation which is  necessary to bring about hyperinflation  what's more possible is  a period of high inflation we're already  seeing that with inflation over central  bank's targets especially in the us  increasingly in the uk and a little bit  in the eurozone there are economic  theorists out there who say that you  need a little bit of inflation after a  sort of one-time  burst of stimulus like we've had and  that may yet prove to be correct it's  one of the most important questions for  the world economy whether the inflation  we're seeing is likely to be persistent  i don't think we're going to see a  period of depression unless the virus  takes a real turn for the worse  in a way that requires no  many more restrictions or consumers to  stay at home again perhaps the highest  risk of a prolonged slowdown  could be in china  it may not be as serious as a depression  but there are various shocks hitting the  economy at the same time right now  the delta variant  means that parts of the country have had  to go into lockdown  you've seen power cuts in part of the  country because of high prices of coal  and provinces rushing to meet strict  national carbon emissions targets  and then the government itself is is  trying to curb  huge amounts of debt in um the property  sector we're starting to see the housing  market slow and you can imagine all of  that creating a bit of a perfect storm  and  growth in china coming off quite  significantly from the high rates of the  past  why is there a shortage of workers and  what can be done  i think that  in 2022 we're really going to find out  uh what is going on in the labor market  people have talked about uh workers need  to stay home to look after their kids  people have talked about uh unemployment  benefits keeping workers out of the  labour market  people talked about people being  worried to go back and work in proximity  to the public during a pandemic or even  in you know packed warehouses and the  like  gradually all these factors should start  to recede the really interesting  question is has there been a  uh shock to how people look at life and  look at their priorities and look at the  world of work  as a result of the pandemic clearly  working from home has changed uh a lot  for people in their and their day-to-day  lives but i don't think we should rule  out the potential that uh uh workers are  just looking at uh at their things like  their work-life balance very differently  and and think about the necessity of  work uh differently and if there's been  a sort of change in people's preferences  during the pandemic then that could also  uh create shortages in the in in the  labor market  too once we know what's causing those  labour shortages once we have a better  idea of what's causing those labour  shortages we might have a better idea of  how to go about resolving them or  governments might have a better idea of  how to go about resolving them  to some extent  wages should start to rise and your  people back into the labour market or  into the industries where demand is  growing really quickly the governments  might need to start thinking about  retraining or linking benefit payments  to returning to work  how are disrupted supply chains  affecting the global economy and what  can governments do  what's happened is there's been this big  surge in demand for goods uh caused by  stimulus and caused by people not going  out as much as they were  and so good's expenditure is much higher  globally than it is normally especially  coming out of america and that has  caused supply chains to run up against  what look like quite hard limits that  aren't going to be able to unwind  themselves at quite the pace that they  did uh in 2020 when you were looking at  reorganizing supply chains to get a  produce ppe and that and that sort of  thing what you have is the world economy  saying we can't quite cope with the  level of goods demand and what needs to  happen is the world economy needs to  shift a bit back more towards spending  on services to get economic growth from  the service sector  rather than pushing more and more  stimulus and more and more spending into  goods where supply chains really need  a bit of time for the investment  in things like shipping and microchips  and so on to enable the greater  production that would satisfy  that demand for goods  what economic policies could governments  introduce to help tackle climate change  the european union is planning to spend  about 750 billion euros  on something called the common recovery  fund which will include plenty of  spending on green infrastructure and  green projects  um  president joe biden in america has also  got got plans would like to pass  um a bill involving spending on um retro  everything from retrofitting homes to  green infrastructure  what you really want though and  something that this  that we've argued for at the economist  is to price carbon  why has economic recovery from the  pandemic been so uneven around the world  there are probably three reasons why  the recovery from the pandemic has been  so uneven  one is that the virus hit various  countries at different times  so the delta variant of the corona virus  for example started out in india made  its way to britain and europe and  america and is now working its way  through southeast asia so those  countries went into lockdown and imposed  various restrictions on activity at  different points of time  we've also seen different rates of  vaccination and um  richer countries have tended to have  higher rates of vaccination and that  that's enabled them to stay open much  longer  and the final reason why recovery from  the pandemic has has been uneven  is because of the extent of stimulus  that we've seen  enacted in a lot of these places so in  america for example we saw huge amounts  of stimulus being unleashed quite early  on and that meant that people have means  to carry on spending even when they were  stuck at home  how was vaccine rollout around the world  impacting the economy  so the level of vaccination determines  the extent to which economy is exposed  to the virus vaccinations have been way  higher in the rich world um overall and  so that's enabled the rich world to uh  uh uh break that link between the spread  of the virus and hospitalizations  whereas um the emerging world isn't in  the position to do that  that said  people  were very worried about the spread of  the delta variant in emerging markets  and the ability of it to disrupt  production but what we've seen  more recently is the delta wave in  emerging markets start to come off a bit  despite the overall lack of vaccination  the other thing i'd say is that even  within rich countries  you have that division  a lot of the american public are still  not vaccinated and that's clearly  interfering with  america's economy in that the spread of  delta has reduced or seems to have  reduced consumer confidence somewhat  is the gap between rich and poor  becoming wider and how should this be  addressed  we've written about how at a global  level what we saw from  1990 to 2010 was two periods of catch-up  where the poor world uh grew uh faster  than the rich world and uh  incomes began to converge we had this  big fall in global inequality as a  result of globalization and  people getting richer especially in  china as a result of their integration  into the global economy  in the 2010s that stalled a little bit  ketchup became slower and then obviously  the pandemic has uh created this divide  between rich and poor because of the  distribution of vaccines and the ability  to offer stimulus to the economy so  there is a sense in which at a global  level progress seems to have slowed  somewhat it's  very important if we want to continue to  reap the gains that we've seen uh since  uh the 1980s and 1990s in terms of  global inequality uh coming down as  people are integrated into the global  labor force that governments resist the  urge to  put up barriers to trade to put up  barriers to immigration to stop the  global economy integrating because that  has been a tremendous force for good in  the lives of billions of people  thank you for watching you can read more  about the world economies recovery from  the pandemic by clicking the link below  and don't forget to subscribe  [Music]  you

